{"name":"Tentacle Pink","hex":"#ffbacd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tentacle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elongated, boneless, flexible organ or limb of some animals, such as the octopus and squid.","One of the glandular hairs on the leaves of certain insectivorous plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move like a tentacle"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tentacle"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tentacle","blocks":[{"text":"In zoology, a tentacle is a flexible, mobile, and elongated organ present in some species of animals, most of them invertebrates. In animal anatomy, tentacles usually occur in one or more pairs. Anatomically, the tentacles of animals work mainly like muscular hydrostats. Most forms of tentacles are used for grasping and feeding. Many are sensory organs, variously receptive to touch, vision, or to the smell or taste of particular foods or threats. Examples of such tentacles are the eyestalks of various kinds of snails. Some kinds of tentacles have both sensory and manipulatory functions."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Cuttlefish.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tentacle","wikidata":"Q660059"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Snail-front-0A.jpg/500px-Snail-front-0A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Snail-front-0A.jpg/1280px-Snail-front-0A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Snail-front-0A.jpg/500px-Snail-front-0A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Front view of land snail showing upper and lower sets of tentacles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnail-front-0A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg/500px-White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg/1280px-White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg/500px-White_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abalone showing pallial tentacles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_abalone_Haliotis_sorenseni.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Parasite140092-fig3_-_FIG11_Nybelinia_basimegacantha_body.png/500px-Parasite140092-fig3_-_FIG11_Nybelinia_basimegacantha_body.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip